        "id": "source-fig68-complete",
        "description": "One tall qualitative liquid surface-layer molecular-force schematic, with exactly five hollow circles numbered 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 down one vertical line. The successive center gaps visibly decrease downwards: gap12 > gap23 > gap34 > gap45. The hand-drawn distances are not measured molecular coordinates or readings. Molecule 1 has two solid force arrows: F_2 upward from its top, and F_3,4,5 downward from its bottom. Their tails touch the corresponding molecule boundary. Molecule 2 has three solid force arrows: F_3 upward from its top; F_1 downward from its lower-left boundary; F_4,5,6 downward from its lower-right boundary. The two downward arrows are separate and unequal drawn lengths. No force arrows are drawn on molecules 3, 4, 5. No axis, scale, tick, velocity, net-force arrow or calibrated magnitude is printed. All five force arrows are solid, not dashed. Their directions express the source attraction/repulsion relationships, not measured force magnitudes. Horizontal segmented liquid texture spans the background; lines are more closely spaced near the upper surface. The uppermost horizontal line identifies the upper liquid boundary; no calibration or exact molecular-row count is assigned to the texture. The printed grouped force labels visibly list 3,4,5 and 4,5,6 without a visible ellipsis; the source prose expands them with ellipsis as sums over the remaining deeper molecules. Modern additions: One uniform affine enlargement of source pixel landmarks, equal-radius clean circles and exact boundary contact for arrows; layout coordinates are not physical values. Native point assertions describe circle centers and subpixel construction endpoints, not extra particles or visible measured points. ASCII mathematical labels replace typographic subscripts, retaining the same indices and force identities. Clean segmented horizontal texture with label/circle clearances regularizes unmeasured background ink, preserving upper-boundary and density-pattern 